<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page2-buying-a-used-car" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Buying a Used Car</title>

  <p>
    Here's our first real example of a game with incomplete information.
  </p>

  <p>
    A customer wants to know whether a used car is worth the price. The salesman knows whether it is,
    and she doesn't. He'd like to sell the car, and he'd also like a reputation for telling the truth,
    so it isn't obvious what he should say or whether she should believe him.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-4-4-setup">
    <title>Section 4.4, Setting Up the Game</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 4.4 from the beginning through the two bulleted lists of strategies</alert>
      (<m>gg</m>, <m>gb</m>, <m>bg</m>, <m>bb</m> for the salesman, then the same four for the customer).
      So you will stop when you reach the paragraph beginning <q>Consider the salesman to be Player 1.</q>
    </p>
    <p>
      Read both lists carefully. The two players use the same four pairs of letters, but the letters
      don't mean the same thing for each of them.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here's the game tree, so you have it nearby while you work through the questions below.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-used-car-tree">
    <caption>
      Buying a used car. The salesman's payoff is listed first. The two dashed
      curves are the customer's information sets: the upper one joins the two
      nodes where the salesman has said <q>good,</q> and the lower one joins
      the two nodes where he has said <q>bad.</q> In each case she cannot tell
      which of the two nodes she is at, because she cannot see Nature's move.
    </caption>
    <image width="100%">
      <shortdescription>
        Game tree for buying a used car, with Nature moving first, then the
        salesman, then the customer, and eight terminal payoffs.
      </shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>
          A game tree with four levels. At the top, a node labeled N for
          Nature has two branches: a left branch labeled g with probability p,
          and a right branch labeled b with probability 1 minus p.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each of Nature's branches leads to a node labeled S for the
          salesman, and each salesman node has two branches labeled g and b,
          standing for what he tells the customer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each of the four salesman branches leads to a node labeled C for the
          customer, and each customer node has two branches labeled g and b,
          standing for what she concludes about the car.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reading the eight terminal payoffs from left to right, with the
          salesman's payoff first, they are: 3 and 1; 1 and 0; 2 and 1; 0 and
          0; 2 and 0; 0 and 1; 3 and 0; 1 and 1.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two dashed curves mark the customer's information sets. The first
          joins the leftmost customer node to the third customer node, which
          are the two nodes reached when the salesman says the car is good.
          The second joins the second customer node to the rightmost customer
          node, which are the two nodes reached when the salesman says the car
          is bad.
        </p>
      </description>
      <latex-image>
        \resizebox{15cm}{!}{%
        \begin{tikzpicture}[
          font=\scriptsize,
          nd/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.3pt}
        ]
        \useasboundingbox (-10.2,0.0) rectangle (10.2,6.7);

        % Nature
        \node[nd] (N) at (0,6) {};
        \node[above=3pt of N] {$N$};

        % Salesman nodes
        \node[nd] (S1) at (-5,4.3) {};
        \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of S1] {$S$};
        \node[nd] (S2) at (5,4.3) {};
        \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of S2] {$S$};

        \draw (N) -- node[above left=0pt and 1pt] {$g$} (S1);
        \draw (N) -- node[above right=0pt and 1pt] {$b$} (S2);
        \node at (-3.1,5.6) {probability $= p$};
        \node at (3.3,5.6) {probability $= 1-p$};

        % Customer nodes
        \node[nd] (C1) at (-7.5,2.6) {};
        \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of C1] {$C$};
        \node[nd] (C2) at (-2.5,2.6) {};
        \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of C2] {$C$};
        \node[nd] (C3) at (2.5,2.6) {};
        \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of C3] {$C$};
        \node[nd] (C4) at (7.5,2.6) {};
        \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of C4] {$C$};

        \draw (S1) -- node[above left=0pt and 1pt] {$g$} (C1);
        \draw (S1) -- node[above right=0pt and 1pt] {$b$} (C2);
        \draw (S2) -- node[above left=0pt and 1pt] {$g$} (C3);
        \draw (S2) -- node[above right=0pt and 1pt] {$b$} (C4);

        % Terminal nodes
        \node (T1) at (-8.8,0.8) {$(3,1)$};
        \node (T2) at (-6.2,0.8) {$(1,0)$};
        \node (T3) at (-3.8,0.8) {$(2,1)$};
        \node (T4) at (-1.2,0.8) {$(0,0)$};
        \node (T5) at (1.2,0.8) {$(2,0)$};
        \node (T6) at (3.8,0.8) {$(0,1)$};
        \node (T7) at (6.2,0.8) {$(3,0)$};
        \node (T8) at (8.8,0.8) {$(1,1)$};

        \draw (C1) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$g$} (T1);
        \draw (C1) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$b$} (T2);
        \draw (C2) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$g$} (T3);
        \draw (C2) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$b$} (T4);
        \draw (C3) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$g$} (T5);
        \draw (C3) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$b$} (T6);
        \draw (C4) -- node[above left=0pt and 0pt] {$g$} (T7);
        \draw (C4) -- node[above right=0pt and 0pt] {$b$} (T8);

        % Information sets
        \draw[dashed] (C1) .. controls (-5.5,3.35) and (0.5,3.35) .. (C3);
        \draw[dashed] (C2) .. controls (0.5,1.85) and (5.5,1.85) .. (C4);
        \end{tikzpicture}
        }
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Right before the strategy lists, the book explains why backward induction doesn't work on this
    game. Let's make sure that landed.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-why-not-backward-induction">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why can't we solve this game with backward induction?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            When the salesman says <q>good,</q> the customer's best move depends on whether the car
            is really good. How likely that is depends on the salesman's strategy, and we haven't
            figured that out yet.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Backward induction solves the bottom of the tree first, but the customer's best
            move at the bottom depends on what the salesman does higher up. If he always tells the
            truth, <q>good</q> means good. If he always lies, <q>good</q> means bad. She can't pick a
            move until she knows which kind of salesman she's dealing with.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because Nature moves first, and backward induction doesn't work once chance is involved.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Chance by itself isn't the problem. If only Nature's moves came before the customer's
            information set, she'd know the chances of being at each node were <m>p</m> and
            <m>1-p</m>, and she could use expected value to pick her move. The trouble is that the
            salesman also makes a choice before she moves.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Because the customer doesn't know <m>p</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Even if she knows <m>p</m> exactly, it isn't enough. The number <m>p</m> tells her how
            likely a car like this is to be good. What she needs is how likely it is to be good
            <em>given that the salesman said so</em>, and that depends on how honest he is.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Since we can't use backward induction, the book converts the game to normal form using the
    strategy lists you just read.
  </p>

  <p>
    First, a quick reminder of what the two letters in a strategy mean:
    <me>
    \text{Salesman: }\;
    \underbrace{\;\square\;}_{\substack{\text{what he says}\\ \text{if the car is good}}}
    \quad
    \underbrace{\;\square\;}_{\substack{\text{what he says}\\ \text{if the car is bad}}}
    </me>
    <me>
    \text{Customer: }\;
    \underbrace{\;\square\;}_{\substack{\text{what she concludes}\\ \text{if he says good}}}
    \quad
    \underbrace{\;\square\;}_{\substack{\text{what she concludes}\\ \text{if he says bad}}}
    </me>
  </p>

  <p>
    Now let's put both players together and follow a strategy profile through the tree.
    Take <m>(gg, gb)</m>, where the salesman always says the car is good and
    the customer always believes him. The salesman is Player 1, so his
    strategy is listed first.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-bad-car-payoffs">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the players use <m>(gg, gb)</m> and Nature makes the car
        <em>bad</em>. What are the payoffs?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(2,0)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. He says the car is good, she believes him and buys it, so
            he collects 2 points for the sale. He doesn't get the truth point,
            because the car was bad. She's wrong about the car, so she gets 0.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(3,0)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            He does sell the car, which is worth 2 points. But the extra point
            is for telling the truth, and he said a bad car was good. Follow
            the <m>b</m> branch out of Nature and then the <m>g</m> branch out
            of <m>S</m> to see this on the tree.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>(0,1)</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is what happens if the customer decides the car is bad and
            walks away. But her strategy here is <m>gb</m>, and he said
            <q>good,</q> so she uses her first letter and concludes the car is
            good.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    That's one of Nature's two branches. The profile <m>(gg, gb)</m> doesn't
    pick out a single path through the tree, though, because Nature could go
    either way. The widget below puts both paths side by side and asks what
    we can say about the payoffs.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-used-car-expected-payoff">
    <caption>
      Both paths that the strategy profile <m>(gg, gb)</m> allows, and what
      they tell us about the salesman's payoff.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-used-car-expected-payoff"
      iframe="ch4-graph/used-car-expected-payoff.html" width="100%" aspect="5:6">
      <static>
        <p>
          A two stage walkthrough built on the used car game tree. The tree is
          redrawn with Nature's branches labeled with probability p on the
          left and probability 1 minus p on the right. Both paths allowed by
          the strategy profile gg for the salesman and gb for the customer are
          highlighted at once: the path in which Nature makes the car good,
          the salesman says good, and the customer concludes good, ending at
          payoffs 3 for the salesman and 1 for the customer; and the path in
          which Nature makes the car bad, the salesman says good, and the
          customer concludes good, ending at payoffs 2 for the salesman and 0
          for the customer.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the first stage, a two choice question asks which statement
          describes the salesman's payoff under this profile. The correct
          statement is that his payoff is 3 with probability p and 2 with
          probability 1 minus p. The incorrect statement reverses the two
          probabilities, giving payoff 3 with probability 1 minus p and 2 with
          probability p. Feedback points back to Nature's branches to show
          which probability goes with which payoff.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pressing Next moves to the second stage, which sets p equal to three
          quarters. The display restates that the salesman's payoff is 3 with
          probability three quarters and 2 with probability one quarter, and
          asks for his expected payoff, recalling the definition of expected
          value from Section 4.1. The two choices are written out as full
          computations. The correct one is three quarters times 3 plus one
          quarter times 2, which equals eleven quarters. The incorrect one is
          one half times 3 plus one half times 2, which equals five halves;
          its arithmetic is right but it weights Nature's two branches equally
          instead of by their probabilities. The stage closes by stating that
          the salesman's expected payoff in this strategy profile is eleven
          quarters.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    You just built the salesman's payoff for one strategy profile out of two
    paths and their probabilities. In other words, his payoff is the
    <em>expected value</em> of his payoffs on the two paths:
    <md>
      <mrow>\text{his expected payoff} \amp = p \cdot (\text{payoff if the car is good}) + (1-p) \cdot (\text{payoff if the car is bad})</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp = p(3) + (1-p)(2) = 2 + p.</mrow>
    </md>
    The same reasoning works for the customer: <m>p(1) + (1-p)(0) = p</m>.
    So the profile <m>(gg, gb)</m> has payoffs <m>(2+p,\, p)</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are sixteen profiles in this game, and every one of them works the
    same way. The rest of Section 4.4 does exactly that and collects the
    results into a payoff matrix. Look for <m>(2+p,\, p)</m> in it!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-4-4-matrix">
    <title>Section 4.4, Building the Payoff Matrix</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Pick up where you stopped and read through the next three payoff matrices.</alert>
      The third matrix is the first one with <m>p</m>'s in it. Stop right after it, just before the
      paragraph beginning <q>Let us assume <m>p \gt \frac{1}{2}</m>.</q> We'll find the Nash equilibria
      together before you finish the reading.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The book writes down the payoff matrix for a good car and the payoff
            matrix for a bad car separately.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The third matrix combines them: <m>p</m> times the first plus
            <m>1-p</m> times the second.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Now let's find the Nash equilibria. We'll assume <m>p \gt \frac{1}{2}</m>, which just means
    the car is usually good. As in Section 3.9, a cell is a Nash equilibrium when both payoffs in it
    are boxed. In the widget below you'll box the customer's best responses one row at a time.
    Some rows may have more than one best response.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-used-car-best-response">
    <caption>
      Finding the customer's best responses in the used car game, then reading
      off the Nash equilibria.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-used-car-best-response"
      iframe="ch4-graph/used-car-best-response.html" width="100%" aspect="5:6">
      <static>
        <p>
          A guided walkthrough of best response boxing on the used car payoff
          matrix, with the salesman as the row player and the customer as the
          column player. The matrix entries, written with the salesman's
          payoff first, are as follows. Row gg: 2 plus p and p; 2 plus p and
          p; p and 1 minus p; p and 1 minus p. Row gb: 3 and p; 1 plus 2p and
          1; 3 minus 2p and 0; 1 and 1 minus p. Row bg: 2 and p; 2 minus 2p
          and 0; 2p and 1; 0 and 1 minus p. Row bb: 3 minus p and p; 1 minus p
          and 1 minus p; 3 minus p and p; 1 minus p and 1 minus p. Throughout,
          p is assumed to be greater than one half.
        </p>
        <p>
          The walkthrough takes the salesman's four strategies one at a time.
          For each, that row is highlighted and the salesman's payoffs in the
          row are dimmed so that only the customer's payoffs stand out. A
          dashed outline appears around the customer's payoff in every cell of
          the row, marking where a box can be drawn. Selecting a cell turns
          that dashed outline into a solid box. The task is to box every cell
          giving the customer her highest payoff in that row, then press Check.
          More than one cell may be correct. After checking, a box that belongs
          turns green, a box drawn where it does not belong turns red, and any
          best response left unboxed appears as a green dashed outline. Boxes
          from rows already completed stay in place as the student moves on, so
          the matrix fills in one row at a time.
        </p>
        <p>
          In row gg the customer's payoffs are p, p, 1 minus p, and 1 minus p,
          so the first two cells tie for best, since p is greater than one
          half. In row gb her payoffs are p, 1, 0, and 1 minus p, so the
          second cell is the unique best. In row bg her payoffs are p, 0, 1,
          and 1 minus p, so the third cell is the unique best. In row bb her
          payoffs are p, 1 minus p, p, and 1 minus p, so the first and third
          cells tie for best. Feedback for rows gg and bb points out that this
          is where the assumption that p is greater than one half is needed,
          and feedback for rows gb and bg points out that no assumption about
          p is needed there.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pressing Next skips the parallel work for the salesman and displays
          the completed matrix with all boxes drawn, on the customer's best
          responses across each row and on the salesman's best responses down
          each column. A final multiple choice question asks which strategy
          profiles are Nash equilibria, that is, which cells have both payoffs
          boxed. The answer is the two profiles gg paired with gb, and bb
          paired with bg.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Notice that <m>(gg, gb)</m> is one of the two equilibria. It's the same
    profile we traced through the tree earlier.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-4-4-conclusion">
    <title>Section 4.4, Finishing Up</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 4.4.</alert> We just worked out the Nash
      equilibria, so you can move quickly through the boxed matrix. The last two
      paragraphs reflect on the results.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-4-2" iframe="question-form.html?section=4.2" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
